WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.022
Ask a question. Count what
people say. Then show it.

2
00:00:08.242 --> 00:00:11.865
A tally, a block graph and
a pictogram all do that job.

3
00:00:12.365 --> 00:00:17.473
Sorting puts things that are alike
together. It needs one clear rule.

4
00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:21.733
Here the rule is fruit, or
vegetable. Nothing else matters.

5
00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:26.086
An apple is a fruit, so it goes
here. A carrot is a vegetable.

6
00:00:26.306 --> 00:00:31.461
Each thing goes in one hoop only.
Sort by the rule, not by colour.

7
00:00:31.961 --> 00:00:35.444
An investigation starts with
a question you want answered.

8
00:00:35.664 --> 00:00:39.008
Which fruit do you like best?
Ask everyone in your class.

9
00:00:39.228 --> 00:00:43.036
Make one mark for every answer
you hear. Do it straight away.

10
00:00:43.256 --> 00:00:48.179
Every fifth mark goes across the
other four. That bundle is five.

11
00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:52.253
Mango got a gate of five, and
three more. That is eight.

12
00:00:52.753 --> 00:00:56.793
Now show your counts on a block
graph. One block is one child.

13
00:00:57.013 --> 00:01:01.286
Count the blocks, do not guess.
The mango column reaches eight.

14
00:01:01.506 --> 00:01:06.521
Banana has five blocks. Orange
has four. They start on one line.

15
00:01:06.741 --> 00:01:10.039
Mango is tallest, so most
children chose mango.

16
00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:14.113
Eight take away five is three,
so three more chose mango.

17
00:01:14.613 --> 00:01:19.211
A pictogram shows the same counts
using pictures instead of blocks.

18
00:01:19.431 --> 00:01:23.610
Here one picture is one child,
so you just count the pictures.

19
00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:28.799
But always read the key first. Here
one picture stands for two children.

20
00:01:29.019 --> 00:01:34.221
Walk has four pictures. Two, four,
six, eight: that is eight children.

21
00:01:34.721 --> 00:01:39.458
A Carroll diagram sorts things two
ways at once. Check both labels.

22
00:01:39.678 --> 00:01:42.743
Across the top it says red, or not red.

23
00:01:42.963 --> 00:01:45.377
Down the side it says round, or not round.

24
00:01:45.597 --> 00:01:50.195
An apple is red and round, so it
goes in the box where both are true.

25
00:01:50.695 --> 00:01:54.596
A coin can land two ways, heads
or tails. Toss it ten times.

26
00:01:54.816 --> 00:01:58.949
This run gave six heads and four
tails. Six and four make ten.

27
00:01:59.169 --> 00:02:03.999
Heads, tails, heads, tails keeps
repeating. That is a regular pattern.

28
00:02:04.219 --> 00:02:08.956
Your real tosses have no repeat
anywhere. That is a random pattern.

29
00:02:09.176 --> 00:02:14.098
So nobody can say how the next
toss will land. Heads is not due.

30
00:02:14.598 --> 00:02:18.128
You sorted by one rule,
and kept a tally in fives.

31
00:02:18.348 --> 00:02:21.877
You read a block graph, and
a pictogram with its key.

32
00:02:22.097 --> 00:02:25.116
A Carroll diagram sorts two ways at once.

33
00:02:25.336 --> 00:02:28.540
And you can tell a regular
pattern from a random one.
